Hi Leslie,

Here is what Keller can provide on your questions.

Alder (1=E2=80=934): The field crew reported that the pavement moved under lo=
ad, and we prioritized the Depot Gate side on that basis. I do not have a com=
parison record for continuing the original allocation, the name of the person=
 who confirmed the limits, or a daily report or acceptance record to provide =
with this response.

Mill (5=E2=80=938): We used a partial-width cut to protect the existing outle=
t and its supporting edge. Leaving the cut full width around the outlet riske=
d damage to those features. The planned 840 square yards includes the approac=
h taper and return as well as the partial-width outlet section. The attached =
schedule describes the three parts together; it does not give a separate area=
 for each part. I am not providing a completed measurement sheet with this re=
sponse.

Quarry (9=E2=80=9312): The field crew judged the gate-approach subbase weak a=
nd gave it priority over the ditch-edge repair. I cannot identify a diagnosti=
c record, the person who confirmed the repair limits, or the accepted extent =
from the material available for this response. I do not have a contemporaneou=
s record of the decision to provide here.

Regards,
Raymond Salazar
Keller Municipal Paving Inc

On July 9, 2025 at 9:38 AM EDT, Leslie Crawford wrote:

> Hi Raymond,
>
> I am examining three FY2023 road contracts and would appreciate Keller's ac=
count of the decisions behind the changes. The page references below are to t=
he Borough's August release. Please respond by 5:00 p.m. EDT on July 16, and =
let me know if another person can help locate the underlying records.
>
> Alder Road, Depot Gate=E2=80=93Linden Avenue: PW-23-01 CO-02, signed Septem=
ber 12, 2023, reassigns surface-course quantity toward the soft area near Dep=
ot Gate (release pp. 46=E2=80=9348).
>
> 1. What observations led the crew to identify that area as soft and in need=
 of priority treatment?
> 2. What comparison was made with continuing under the original allocation, =
and what would that alternative have meant?
> 3. Who measured and confirmed the limits of the soft area and the proposed =
treatment?
> 4. Which contemporaneous daily report or acceptance record records the deci=
sion and work, and may we see it?
>
> Mill Road, Stone Culvert=E2=80=93Pump House Lane: the August 29, 2023 revis=
ed quantity schedule for PW-23-02 changes the outlet layout from full-width t=
o partial-width milling and lists 840 square yards as the planned area, inclu=
ding transitions (release pp. 61=E2=80=9364).
>
> 5. Why was a full-width cut not retained around the outlet?
> 6. What effect did Keller expect if full-width milling remained there?
> 7. How were the approach taper, the partial-width outlet section and the re=
turn laid out, and how was the 840-square-yard estimate derived?
> 8. Where is the completed measurement sheet for the actual area, and may we=
 see it?
>
> Quarry Road, Weighhouse Gate=E2=80=93Briar Street: PW-23-03 CO-01, signed O=
ctober 3, 2023, shifts 400 square yards of planned repair from the ditch edge=
 to localized subbase repair on the gate approach (release pp. 95=E2=80=9399).
>
> 9. What observations led the field crew to judge the approach subbase deter=
iorated?
> 10. How did Keller weigh that work against retaining the planned ditch-edge=
 repair?
> 11. Who confirmed the limits of the subbase repair and how were they establ=
ished?
> 12. What record made at the time supports the decision, and may we see it?
>
> Thank you,
> Leslie Crawford
